  "title": "Product Marginals and Density-Moment Thresholds for Ising Transfer Kernels",
  "abstract": "We compare exact marginal agreement and density-moment control for finite Boolean Ising transfer operators with global weights. Code-based weights preserve uniform labelled marginals on a positive fraction of coordinates while exponentially many normalized transfer eigenvalues approach one. Separate constructions and hypercontractive estimates determine the critical norm-budget boundary and a sharp strict-tail threshold. At the critical exponent s=(1+rho)/(1-rho), positive densities under the exact norm cap 2^(1/s), with any prescribed density floor below one, can match every marginal through k_L=o(sqrt(L)) while their first two transfer eigenvalues tend to one. Conversely, any bounded critical norm and k_L/sqrt(L) tending to infinity imply operator-norm convergence of S_r=P_sqrt(rho) M_r P_sqrt(rho) to P_rho and spectral-gap convergence for the isospectral transfer congruence T_r. Norm convergence of T_r itself is not asserted. An entropy-deficit argument removes the logarithmic loss and identifies the exact vanishing scale for the endpoint high-degree noise norm. At fixed k_L/sqrt(L) tending to C>0, odd Fourier erasure and a one-sided positivity estimate yield an explicit attained gap profile with a quadratic small-C upper bound. At the same critical cap and with a fixed positive density floor 0<b<1, a mean-reserved tail-moment budget and three-degree-block decoupling prove a uniform positive normalized gap for every fixed C>0. For each such fixed floor, the lower and attained upper bounds give quadratic order of the asymptotic gap infimum as C decreases to zero, after the dimension limit; optimal constants remain open. The code, tilt and spike families are distinct; external priority is unestablished. No fixed-range Gibbs, local-dynamics, physical-gap or continuum conclusion follows.",
